{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And when you reap, etc.</b> The reason He began speaking in plural language and finished speaking in singular language, <b>you shall not complete</b>, is to negate the view of those who err and say that when there is not enough for the poor, one is not obligated to give — on the order of what he said (II Kings 4:43): ״What should I give this before a hundred״ etc. Therefore HaShem commanded in singular language, to say that even one poor person alone obligates him in pe'ah, even though the pe'ah of a single person is but little for all the poor of Israel. And likewise regarding the commandments of leket (gleaning) and peret (fallen grapes) and olelot (defective clusters), He spoke in singular language for the aforementioned reason." "And the reason for the juxtaposition of this commandment to the punishment of karet — perhaps it hints that a person should not say: if he sinned deliberately and incurred karet, from now on there is no need to guard himself from whatever evil deed may come before him, since he has already incurred karet. Therefore He said: <b>And when you reap the harvest of your land</b> — meaning, when he caused it to come about that the harvest of his land was cut down, which is the karet mentioned in proximity — <b>you shall not complete the corner of your field to reap</b> it in other evil deeds. And by this He informed him that not the entire soul is cut off but only the branch in which the commandment alluded to here is rooted, and it still remains for him to see others attached to their root, for every soul of Israel has roots on high corresponding to all the commandments of the Torah. And He said <b>and the gleaning of your harvest</b> etc. — meaning, even in the branch of the sin itself, do not add further sinning. For you should know the statement of the Rav the Arizal, of blessed memory, that it is in the nature of holiness to leave a trace in the place where it has been. And if so, even though he was cut off, there still remains a residue of the harvest there, and this is what He commanded concerning it: <b>and the gleaning of your harvest you shall not glean</b> in an abundance of transgressions — for HaShem desires that through the medium of that very leket he be aroused and return in repentance before HaShem, and He will have mercy upon him. For will you not find that they said, of blessed memory (Yoma 86a), that even if a person transgressed all the karet offenses and death-penalty offenses of the court, repentance etc. atones? And as the prophet said (Hosea 14:2): ״Return, etc. unto HaShem your Elohim״, and they interpreted, of blessed memory (ibid.): Great is repentance, for it reaches the Throne of Glory — meaning, that even though the thread connecting to the Throne of Glory was cut and only a small portion remained within him, repentance reaches him to the Throne of Glory."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:58.142247825Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}